267
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,710
just entered grade one.

268
00:12:04,710 --> 00:12:05,790
And the thought of like knowing

269
00:12:05,790 --> 00:12:08,370
that her entire educational journey is gonna be altered

270
00:12:08,370 --> 00:12:10,800
because of this new technology is fascinating to me,

271
00:12:10,800 --> 00:12:13,560
and I wanna influence that as much as I can

272
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:14,670
in like a very positive way.

273
00:12:14,670 --> 00:12:15,960
So that's kind of why I'm here

274
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:17,610
and the driver for me being here.

275
00:12:18,780 --> 00:12:20,340
- Hi, everybody, I'm Jessica Lopez.

276
00:12:20,340 --> 00:12:22,710
I am a student.

277
00:12:22,710 --> 00:12:25,320
I was born without hands and feet, as you can see,

278
00:12:25,320 --> 00:12:26,850
but I also developed a chronic illness

279
00:12:26,850 --> 00:12:27,870
when I was 10 years old

280
00:12:27,870 --> 00:12:30,810
that is not related to my physical disability,

281
00:12:30,810 --> 00:12:35,810
which completely changed and altered the course of my life.

282
00:12:36,180 --> 00:12:37,780
Before I became chronically ill,

283
00:12:37,780 --> 00:12:39,390
I was at a performing arts school.

284
00:12:39,390 --> 00:12:40,830
I did singing, dancing, and drama.

285
00:12:40,830 --> 00:12:42,420
I was very physically active.

286
00:12:42,420 --> 00:12:44,040
I used to ride horses,

287
00:12:44,040 --> 00:12:47,207
and my chronic illness completely stopped all of that.

288
00:12:47,207 --> 00:12:49,800
And so I ended up graduating high school

289
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:52,560
four years later than I should have because of that.

290
00:12:52,560 --> 00:12:54,750
The only reason why I was able to graduate

291
00:12:54,750 --> 00:12:56,670
was because of the advancement

292
00:12:56,670 --> 00:12:58,380
of online education in COVID.

293
00:12:58,380 --> 00:13:00,117
I was able to complete two years of high school

294
00:13:00,117 --> 00:13:01,980
in less than four months

295
00:13:01,980 --> 00:13:05,190
because I was able to study online,

296
00:13:05,190 --> 00:13:09,080
study at a pace that is more efficient for my body

297
00:13:09,080 --> 00:13:11,460
and log off, and I'm not able to do that.

298
00:13:11,460 --> 00:13:13,320
And that completely changed my life.

299
00:13:13,320 --> 00:13:14,210
I found Coastline College,

300
00:13:14,210 --> 00:13:16,830
which is an online community college,

301
00:13:16,830 --> 00:13:20,250
and really was able to kind of expand my opportunities.

302
00:13:20,250 --> 00:13:22,020
I became president of student government,

303
00:13:22,020 --> 00:13:24,990
really advocating for the advancement of online education

304
00:13:24,990 --> 00:13:27,300
and making sure that it doesn't disappear

305
00:13:27,300 --> 00:13:30,060
as we go further and further away from the pandemic.

306
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:32,970
I graduated with three associate degrees in two years

307
00:13:32,970 --> 00:13:34,890
in business, economics, and communications,

308
00:13:34,890 --> 00:13:36,480
and then I transferred to Arizona State,

309
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:38,460
which is also online,

310
00:13:38,460 --> 00:13:42,060
where I'm advocating for disability inclusion in general

311
00:13:42,060 --> 00:13:45,720
and making sure that any technology that we use

312
00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:48,660
is benefiting people with disabilities

313
00:13:48,660 --> 00:13:51,870
and making sure that people with the most rare disabilities

314
00:13:51,870 --> 00:13:54,450
can still access things and have the same opportunity

315
00:13:54,450 --> 00:13:55,820
that I was able to get myself

316
00:13:55,820 --> 00:13:59,730
as a result of systemic barriers being eliminated

317
00:13:59,730 --> 00:14:02,220
through just the way society decided

318
00:14:02,220 --> 00:14:03,900
to operate within COVID.

319
00:14:03,900 --> 00:14:05,460
So yeah, that's a little bit about me.

320
00:14:05,460 --> 00:14:09,000
- And I just wanna... (audience applauding)

321
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:12,060
I just wanna emphasize something from Jessica's bio.

322
00:14:12,060 --> 00:14:15,030
She got three associate degrees in two years.

323
00:14:15,030 --> 00:14:18,020
I mean, it's phenomenal. (audience applauding)

324
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:20,460
And with the right opportunities, right?

325
00:14:20,460 --> 00:14:21,510
The right opportunities,

326
00:14:21,510 --> 00:14:23,850
like having the online education available to her

327
00:14:23,850 --> 00:14:26,400
during COVID to catch up on her education.

328
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:28,590
So these are really critical things.

329
00:14:28,590 --> 00:14:31,680
My next question is for our students a little bit more.

330
00:14:31,680 --> 00:14:32,820
I'll start with Eden.

331
00:14:32,820 --> 00:14:34,720
Eden, you talked a little bit about your disability.

332
00:14:34,720 --> 00:14:36,740
Can you talk a bit about

333
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:39,870
what are the challenges you faced in your coursework?

334
00:14:39,870 --> 00:14:42,030
I know you mentioned writing.

335
00:14:42,030 --> 00:14:43,470
Just as a quick preface to that,

336
00:14:43,470 --> 00:14:47,010
I just met Eden in facilitating this panel

337
00:14:47,010 --> 00:14:48,220
and I needed a bio from her

338
00:14:48,220 --> 00:14:50,520
and she was struggling to write.

339
00:14:50,520 --> 00:14:52,170
I didn't know about her disability.

340
00:14:52,170 --> 00:14:54,180
And so she was struggling to write that bio

341
00:14:54,180 --> 00:14:56,730
and then she called me and we had a 20-minute conversation

342
00:14:56,730 --> 00:14:58,650
and I got everything I needed from her.

343
00:14:58,650 --> 00:15:00,570
But previous to that, she told me she spent three

344
00:15:00,570 --> 00:15:03,087
or four hours trying to put together a written bio for me.

345
00:15:03,087 --> 00:15:04,860
And I had asked her something,

346
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:06,390
not knowing of that disability,

347
00:15:06,390 --> 00:15:08,370
but really this alternate form was quicker

348
00:15:08,370 --> 00:15:10,530
and it was satisfactory.

349
00:15:10,530 --> 00:15:11,820
So Eden, tell me a little bit more,

350
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:14,733
tell us a little bit more about the challenges you faced.

351
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:19,920
- When I first started my college journey,

352
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:22,170
it was way back in the '70s.

353
00:15:22,170 --> 00:15:23,370
And at that time,

354
00:15:23,370 --> 00:15:27,180
I don't think there was even a word of accommodations.

355
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:28,780
And I struggled with that.

356
00:15:28,780 --> 00:15:33,600
Unfortunately, it was futile.

357
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:35,640
That's how I got my nursing degree.

358
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:40,640
I was one course short of getting my bachelor's degree.

359
00:15:41,130 --> 00:15:44,490
So I left because nobody understood

360
00:15:44,490 --> 00:15:47,400
because they thought, you know, it's in your mind.

361
00:15:47,400 --> 00:15:50,790
You know, that's what my professor said, time management.

362
00:15:50,790 --> 00:15:54,360
But if you think about what ADHD is,

363
00:15:54,360 --> 00:15:57,033
that's disorganization and all that.

364
00:15:58,770 --> 00:16:02,100
And this newly diagnosed for me

365
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:05,040
was the disorder of written expression.

366
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:09,820
Then a couple of years later in the '90s,

367
00:16:09,820 --> 00:16:12,093
now I think it became a word.

368
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:16,720
And fortunately, I came across a blurb

369
00:16:17,850 --> 00:16:21,860
on a school schedule from De Anza that just said,

370
00:16:21,860 --> 00:16:24,390
do you have problem taking notes?

371
00:16:24,390 --> 00:16:27,240
Do you have problems processing?

372
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:28,773
And that opened my door.

373
00:16:29,970 --> 00:16:34,380
And I went to the learning center and I got diagnosed.

374
00:16:34,380 --> 00:16:38,820
I mean, well, they told me that there was something there,

375
00:16:38,820 --> 00:16:40,480
which I think is an eye-opener

376
00:16:40,480 --> 00:16:44,160
because many of our students don't know

377
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:46,140
that they do have a problem.

378
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:48,840
And I think it's important also for administrators,

379
00:16:48,840 --> 00:16:52,800
maybe just put a blurb in your ads

380
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:57,800
to at least go to the DSPS or your disability services

381
00:16:59,400 --> 00:17:03,640
to at least have a student inquire,

382
00:17:04,640 --> 00:17:06,903
is there really something wrong?

383
00:17:08,010 --> 00:17:10,950
Then after that, unfortunately,

384
00:17:10,950 --> 00:17:14,370
due to other circumstances, I had to stop.

385
00:17:14,370 --> 00:17:19,370
And then my third chance was admission.

386
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:22,770
And that also opened doors

387
00:17:22,770 --> 00:17:27,060
because by now, technology kind of caught up.

388
00:17:27,060 --> 00:17:30,630
And that's when I learned about the SmartPen,

389
00:17:30,630 --> 00:17:34,950
which helped me, okay, I don't have to,

390
00:17:34,950 --> 00:17:37,320
because I also have an auditory,

391
00:17:37,320 --> 00:17:39,720
what's that, processing problem.

392
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:42,993
Just listening and writing,

393
00:17:43,830 --> 00:17:45,840
I can't do that at the same time.

394
00:17:45,840 --> 00:17:49,470
So with the SmartPen, that opened another doors.

395
00:17:49,470 --> 00:17:53,100
And then another technology was CurseWild

396
00:17:53,100 --> 00:17:57,960
because I have difficulty in looking at white print.

397
00:17:57,960 --> 00:18:01,170
So there's the dark screen and all that.

398
00:18:01,170 --> 00:18:06,170
Other than that, so with all these disabilities

399
00:18:06,300 --> 00:18:09,390
that I have encountered now,

400
00:18:09,390 --> 00:18:12,320
I also been able to use that knowledge

401
00:18:12,320 --> 00:18:16,143
for tutoring my students with the disabilities.

402
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:20,160
I'm known to be very patient.

403
00:18:20,160 --> 00:18:24,720
And that's the thing about the invisible,

404
00:18:24,720 --> 00:18:26,190
the intellectual disability.

405
00:18:26,190 --> 00:18:27,140
Nobody knows.

406
00:18:27,140 --> 00:18:30,810
If you talk to me, you wouldn't have known.

407
00:18:30,810 --> 00:18:32,640
Even like Marina, she wouldn't have known

408
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:34,023
unless I disclose it.

409
00:18:35,130 --> 00:18:37,680
And so amongst your students,

410
00:18:37,680 --> 00:18:42,540
I think we have to be kind of intuitive

411
00:18:42,540 --> 00:18:44,560
as to if you're in class,

412
00:18:44,560 --> 00:18:47,790
why is this student struggling?

413
00:18:47,790 --> 00:18:50,283
Why are their grades not up to par?

414
00:18:51,140 --> 00:18:53,160
They may not know it,

415
00:18:53,160 --> 00:18:58,160
but you can at least give them an opportunity for that.

416
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:04,620
Another thing before, I have a student

417
00:19:04,620 --> 00:19:09,450
and this is with talking about maybe policies.

418
00:19:09,450 --> 00:19:13,890
Students nowadays, especially like with English,

419
00:19:13,890 --> 00:19:16,890
like in English where they have to write an essay.

420
00:19:16,890 --> 00:19:21,450
A lot of our students are using either chat GPT

421
00:19:21,450 --> 00:19:24,810
or just even text, what's that, text to speech?

422
00:19:24,810 --> 00:19:26,733
Oh no, speech to text.

423
00:19:27,780 --> 00:19:32,780
This student is so afraid of putting what was on the phone

424
00:19:34,530 --> 00:19:37,560
because of, am I plagiarizing?

425
00:19:37,560 --> 00:19:40,880
Because I go, well, that's part of your accommodations.

426
00:19:40,880 --> 00:19:45,360
And they said, but my professor says I can't use that.

427
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:47,460
So imagine with that student,

428
00:19:47,460 --> 00:19:51,960
that's the only way that student can express themself.

429
00:19:51,960 --> 00:19:55,920
So please keep in mind that there are,

430
00:19:55,920 --> 00:19:59,610
maybe when you have policies and stuff that there are,

431
00:19:59,610 --> 00:20:02,940
even with older adults, we struggle too.

432
00:20:02,940 --> 00:20:05,550
So keep that in mind.

433
00:20:05,550 --> 00:20:06,900
Thank you.

434
00:20:06,900 --> 00:20:07,733
- Thanks, Eden.

435
00:20:07,733 --> 00:20:09,240
And Jessica, tell us a little bit about,

436
00:20:09,240 --> 00:20:10,410
obviously you've talked a little bit

437
00:20:10,410 --> 00:20:12,360
about your physical disabilities.

438
00:20:12,360 --> 00:20:14,070
Even coming here today, we were like,

439
00:20:14,070 --> 00:20:17,310
is there gonna be a ramp in the room for her to use?

440
00:20:17,310 --> 00:20:19,230
What is the seating situation?

441
00:20:19,230 --> 00:20:20,730
Where is the elevator?

442
00:20:20,730 --> 00:20:22,830
These are obvious questions that you have to navigate.

443
00:20:22,830 --> 00:20:24,630
But tell us more about the coursework

444
00:20:24,630 --> 00:20:27,390
and some of the experiences you've had there.

445
00:20:27,390 --> 00:20:29,370
- Yeah, I think one of the biggest challenges

446
00:20:29,370 --> 00:20:34,280
for online education is you're kind of by yourself.

447
00:20:34,280 --> 00:20:37,260
And I think a lot of that is the way professors

448
00:20:37,260 --> 00:20:38,580
see online education.

449
00:20:38,580 --> 00:20:40,590
They think of it as like Netflix.

450
00:20:40,590 --> 00:20:42,300
You just go and consume content,

451
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:44,070
and then you do your homework by yourself,

452
00:20:44,070 --> 00:20:45,180
and that's how you learn.

453
00:20:45,180 --> 00:20:48,690
But there is a wide variety of platforms

454
00:20:48,690 --> 00:20:50,850
where you don't have to be learning by yourself.

455
00:20:50,850 --> 00:20:53,430
You can use Zoom, you can make your classes synchronous

456
00:20:53,430 --> 00:20:55,380
instead of asynchronous.

457
00:20:55,380 --> 00:20:58,080
And that's really important in core subjects

458
00:20:58,080 --> 00:21:00,480
where it can be really challenging for a student to learn,

459
00:21:00,480 --> 00:21:01,920
like math or English.

460
00:21:01,920 --> 00:21:04,290
For me, my hardest subject was math.

461
00:21:04,290 --> 00:21:06,720
And so when I was an online student,

462
00:21:06,720 --> 00:21:09,320
it was very challenging for me to just read a textbook

463
00:21:09,320 --> 00:21:10,800
and make it make sense.

464
00:21:10,800 --> 00:21:13,380
I had too many questions and nobody to ask.

465
00:21:13,380 --> 00:21:14,700
I could email my professor

466
00:21:14,700 --> 00:21:17,200
and ask him clarifying questions,

467
00:21:17,200 --> 00:21:19,485
but I need those answers right now

468
00:21:19,485 --> 00:21:21,990
while my brain is in that mode and I can understand it,

469
00:21:21,990 --> 00:21:25,240
not his delayed response three days later.

470
00:21:25,240 --> 00:21:27,570
And I think that's one of the most important things

471
00:21:27,570 --> 00:21:29,910
about online education is that there's a way

472
00:21:29,910 --> 00:21:33,330
of having office hours where people can drop in

473
00:21:33,330 --> 00:21:36,750
or having synchronous Zoom sessions every week.

474
00:21:36,750 --> 00:21:40,410
Even if your class is asynchronous, still offering that.

475
00:21:40,410 --> 00:21:42,450
My best professors did that,

476
00:21:42,450 --> 00:21:44,280
especially I took remedial math.

477
00:21:44,280 --> 00:21:46,530
I was really nervous about that

478
00:21:46,530 --> 00:21:49,350
because I do know what online education is like,

479
00:21:49,350 --> 00:21:51,210
but my professor would teach us three

480
00:21:51,210 --> 00:21:52,590
hours a week over Zoom

481
00:21:52,590 --> 00:21:54,210
and I was able to learn that way.

482
00:21:54,210 --> 00:21:57,600
But in classes where I wasn't able to have that,

483
00:21:57,600 --> 00:21:59,850
I love with ChatGPT to be like,

484
00:21:59,850 --> 00:22:01,530
this is statistics and I don't

485
00:22:01,530 --> 00:22:02,363
understand this math problem.

486
00:22:02,363 --> 00:22:05,400
ChatGPT, can you answer my questions?

487
00:22:05,400 --> 00:22:06,870
And it would really help me

488
00:22:06,870 --> 00:22:08,790
kind of figure out these math problems

489
00:22:08,790 --> 00:22:12,840
and show me why do I take this path for this math problem

490
00:22:12,840 --> 00:22:14,580
versus that path for another problem.

491
00:22:14,580 --> 00:22:16,650
And so I think that's really important

492
00:22:16,650 --> 00:22:19,380
is leveraging all the tools together

493
00:22:19,380 --> 00:22:22,200
to make sure that you're building a classroom

494
00:22:22,200 --> 00:22:25,110
that is holistic and also thinking about

495
00:22:25,110 --> 00:22:27,870
how disabilities go into it as well.

496
00:22:27,870 --> 00:22:31,470
When I was younger, captioning was not really a thing

497
00:22:31,470 --> 00:22:34,320
I'm hard of hearing and so I need captioning

498
00:22:34,320 --> 00:22:36,880
in order to kind of keep up and follow.

499
00:22:36,880 --> 00:22:40,050
But like when I was younger and like if you watch TV,

500
00:22:40,050 --> 00:22:42,330
captioning wasn't really available.

501
00:22:42,330 --> 00:22:45,860
But over time as things have gone more progressive

502
00:22:45,860 --> 00:22:48,780
and especially AI has been around,

503
00:22:48,780 --> 00:22:51,630
captioning is more widely available than ever before.

504
00:22:51,630 --> 00:22:54,420
And so professors can leverage AI tools

505
00:22:54,420 --> 00:22:56,790
to create captioning that is accurate

506
00:22:56,790 --> 00:23:00,750
and can show the inflection point of people's vocals

507
00:23:00,750 --> 00:23:02,790
and give all that context of people

508
00:23:02,790 --> 00:23:04,200
who are hard of hearing who need that.

509
00:23:04,200 --> 00:23:07,860
And I appreciate my professors who put that effort into it

510
00:23:07,860 --> 00:23:09,510
because I do know it takes time

511
00:23:09,510 --> 00:23:10,890
but that makes the difference

512
00:23:10,890 --> 00:23:12,870
between a student who can actually learn

513
00:23:12,870 --> 00:23:14,940
and a student who has to dump their class

514
00:23:14,940 --> 00:23:16,290
and try to find another professor

515
00:23:16,290 --> 00:23:18,090
willing to give them what they need.

516
00:23:19,170 --> 00:23:20,190
- Thanks, Jessica.

517
00:23:20,190 --> 00:23:21,870
All right, so Keelan and Eddie,

518
00:23:21,870 --> 00:23:24,120
you've heard Jessica and Eden talk a little bit

519
00:23:24,120 --> 00:23:25,650
about some of their challenges.

520
00:23:25,650 --> 00:23:29,040
Where do you see industry partners coming in

521
00:23:29,040 --> 00:23:32,910
potentially using AI to support some of these needs?

522
00:23:32,910 --> 00:23:35,040
- The first thing that comes to mind is personalization.

523
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:36,960
And what you brought up that bringing a lot of tools

524
00:23:36,960 --> 00:23:39,090
coming together is super important.

525
00:23:39,090 --> 00:23:40,977
I get a chance to go to a lot of campuses around the US

526
00:23:40,977 --> 00:23:43,380
and sort of hear from teachers, professors,

527
00:23:43,380 --> 00:23:45,180
what are they trying to solve and what can they help with?

528
00:23:45,180 --> 00:23:48,540
And a lot of it comes down to that there's edge case

529
00:23:48,540 --> 00:23:51,030
where students can't either understand the curriculum,

530
00:23:51,030 --> 00:23:52,170
they can't interact with it.

531
00:23:52,170 --> 00:23:54,990
And that's where AI can really make a huge play.

532
00:23:54,990 --> 00:23:57,210
